{
    "title": "Modifying the Scope of Tariffs on the Government of Brazil",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "November 26, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-26/pdf/2025-21417.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has narrowed the Brazilian tariff policy it set this summer, carving out many agricultural goods and a few other items from the extra 40 percent duty. The change is already in effect for shipments arriving on or after November 13, and Customs will issue refunds where the higher charge was collected.\n\nThis move follows a call between the U.S. and Brazilian presidents and the start of negotiations to address the dispute. The order updates the tariff schedule and expands the list of products not subject to the surcharge. The exemptions focus on food and farm imports such as beef, fruit, nuts, coffee, spices, and juices, along with some coconut water and a\u00e7a\u00ed products used in beverages. Several civil\u2011aircraft parts and a limited set of industrial materials are also listed. Agencies are directed to keep monitoring the situation and recommend further changes if needed.\n\nImporters, grocery chains, restaurants, and beverage brands that rely on these Brazilian ingredients stand to benefit first, because their costs should ease. Consumers could see more stable prices on specific foods over time. Brazilian producers of the newly exempt goods gain renewed access to the U.S. market without the added charge, which may help keep supply flowing while talks continue. Companies that paid the extra duty on now\u2011exempt items should get refunds through the normal Customs process.\n\nOn the other hand, U.S. farmers and ranchers who compete directly with Brazilian beef and produce could face tougher price competition as the tariff shield comes down for those items. Importers of Brazilian products that remain under the 40 percent duty will still carry higher costs and uncertainty, since the broader national\u2011emergency tariff remains in place and could change again as negotiations progress.\n\nOverall, the order signals a tactical adjustment rather than an end to the dispute: keep pressure where Washington sees risk, relieve it where talks show progress, and leave room to move in either direction as the two countries continue to negotiate.",
